<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tetouan under al-Mandri</h1><p class="st-meta">1484 CE – 1567 CE (approx.) · capital Tetouan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tetouan refounded by exiles from Granada under the al-Mandri family, a corsair city ruled for a time by the famous Sayyida al-Hurra.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> had lain in ruins since a Castilian raid around 1400. Around 1484, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-al-mandri">Ali al-Mandri</a>, a military leader from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada</a>, rebuilt the town with Andalusi refugees, with permission from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wattasid-sultanate">Wattasid Sultanate</a>, as a base against the Portuguese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Andalusi elite ran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> as an autonomous city, rebuilding walls and the medina in Granadan style. Corsairs operating from its river raided Spanish and Portuguese shipping and coasts, bringing captives for ransom. After the death of her husband, a Mandri ruler of the city (sources differ on whether he was the founder or a kinsman), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyida-al-hurra">Sayyida al-Hurra</a>, daughter of the founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-chefchaouen">Emirate of Chefchaouen</a>, governed from about 1515 to 1542 and dealt directly with Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyida-al-hurra">Sayyida al-Hurra</a> was deposed in 1542, and the Mandri family's influence waned as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> extended its control over northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> in the 1560s. The Andalusi heritage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan's</a> medina, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage</a> site, remains.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1484 CE – 1567 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.23%;width:1.65%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1484 CE – 1567 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tetouan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali al-Mandri, Sayyida al-Hurra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Tetouan, Mandri lordship</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=tetouan-under-al-mandri" aria-label="Tetouan under al-Mandri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4544 892 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tetouan under al-Mandri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4864 1148L4910 1095M4864 1148L4873 1200M4864 1148L4873 1200"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 1095h0M4873 1200h0M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4864 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4864 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tetouan. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=tetouan-under-al-mandri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
